e following picture:\n\n![](images/c2/c272ff1454ac4ab9825e312f563c9bca08ac2800faa0e95ab45e8a5e827369da.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393556 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105787 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "49c501da-161c-53be-8349-571cc6a07975" :knowledge-point/title "Plotting the Product of a Matrix and an Eigenvector Given its Corresponding Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20d82492-3855-57ba-b3fa-259a3e6e9203" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c0/c0bffb825fa5610288e3c88555a41c8124cf3538b2f75ebe627543a678ca3fba.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393558} {:answer/id #uuid "840f7119-572d-50c5-bbbf-83c0cac108a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c4/c42bc75b2c46989d81d20a9025ee8e487408f06576ec4a00115f20891cf5a824.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393559} {:answer/id #uuid "40d3edc5-becc-58a4-934d-50f6796ae78b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/09/09b44673879a2991acb4d5cdba4fad6233a87035b51cd9aa090ee9445ba5fa42.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393560} {:answer/id #uuid "95376528-e4b3-531b-86b5-3f2b9c559f52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/47/47f95dca66badab7adf57a36680877109c2d918210a1a412fd4e6532d973cd61.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393561} {:answer/id #uuid "d5663d4b-636b-5cde-93d7-c48645e55519" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d4/d4d87620b1b1f7ec62825bbd532998595455f601db2ba7c841e0a0167dff4fb4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393562}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20d82492-3855-57ba-b3fa-259a3e6e9203" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c0/c0bffb825fa5610288e3c88555a41c8124cf3538b2f75ebe627543a678ca3fba.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393558} :answer-field/id #uuid "d944ba04-8e3b-5f93-b26b-2c144908ea48" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393557}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9be8ba94-ab90-5127-ba65-2ca25adc7b85" :question/math-academy-id "q-75737" :question/problem "![](images/53/539b08ea12f9af52defd826c0b63c194f36b6de408770c1bc1975bd6ca84724f.png)\n\nThe vector $u$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $B$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $-\\frac{1}{2}.$ Find the vector $Bu.$" :question/worked-solution "The eigenvector $u=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Bu & =-\\frac{1}{2}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following picture:\n\n![](images/c0/c0bffb825fa5610288e3c88555a41c8124cf3538b2f75ebe627543a678ca3fba.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345133 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096635 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a7eb3fea-e4cf-5537-9a33-06b9d2b35c6b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System Containing Fractions in Both Equations Using Elimination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "703429e4-f6ee-5ae0-b54b-621a3b3aa87c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-2,\\,y=-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343202} {:answer/id #uuid "a374971b-c760-5d83-adbf-3b1936e47374" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1,\\,y=-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343673} {:answer/id #uuid "bbd32382-778d-546c-b434-bc2f6f15182c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-4,\\,y=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343889} {:answer/id #uuid "bd80722c-1778-5d31-a005-82a0a1a70cf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{2},\\,y=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343905} {:answer/id #uuid "f93e23d5-adfb-539c-932c-d93d3f7f5111" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=4,\\,y=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344431}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd80722c-1778-5d31-a005-82a0a1a70cf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{2},\\,y=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343905} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e62a385-bb95-5754-89d7-c5b01db0db60" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344523}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "263b5643-f9a7-58b5-b4f2-be4d8aef81b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-75862" :question/problem "Find the solution to the  following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x-\\frac{1}{3}y=2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{4}y=1\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To get rid of fractions, we multiply the first equation by $3$ and the second equation by $4,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x-\\frac{1}{3}y=2 & |\\times 3 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{4}y=1 & |\\times 4\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}6x-y=6 \\\\ 2x-y=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that if we multiply the second equation by $-1,$ then we will get a $y$ term that can cancel with the $-y$ term in the first equation:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}6x-y=6 &  \\\\ 2x-y=4 & |\\,\\times (-1)\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}6x-y=6 \\\\ -2x+y=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $y$-variable, and allows us to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x-\\cancel{y} & =6 \\\\ -2x+\\cancel{y} & =-4 \\\\ (6x-2x) & =(6-4) \\\\ 4x & =2 \\\\ x & =\\frac{2}{4} \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ into the second equation to solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-y & =4 \\\\ 2(\\frac{1}{2})-y & =4 \\\\ 1-y & =4 \\\\ y & =1-4 \\\\ y & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=\\frac{1}{2},\\,y=-3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345134 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068880 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5916154-14b6-5e2d-b33c-176b76293927" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations Containing One Distinct Linear Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "403fead1-28dc-51e8-883e-1ea78fb58c8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342768} {:answer/id #uuid "51524fcb-5371-5134-98fd-7bc0b95c2398" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342910} {:answer/id #uuid "6d345723-89f4-5351-84cf-b5bdbe44c4c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343174} {:answer/id #uuid "bcca82d0-a051-5fde-94e1-fbf46764a754" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343898} {:answer/id #uuid "dce93094-4d77-5ba6-b201-1d213789909e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344178}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d345723-89f4-5351-84cf-b5bdbe44c4c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343174} :answer-field/id #uuid "db497c06-807c-5ce4-a68f-3dd5b362ea6c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344873}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "19f3a0d4-a9e3-5752-9f3d-c41b8d14446d" :question/math-academy-id "q-75924" :question/problem "Solve for $x$ where $\\frac{x+7}{x}-\\frac{1-x}{x}=4.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that $x=0$ is an excluded solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nWe multiply both sides of the equation by $x,$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+7}{x}-\\frac{1-x}{x} & =4 \\\\ x\\cdot (\\frac{x+7}{x}-\\frac{1-x}{x}) & =x\\cdot 4 \\\\ x\\cdot \\frac{x+7}{x}-x\\cdot \\frac{1-x}{x} & =x\\cdot 4 \\\\ \\cancel{x}\\cdot \\frac{x+7}{\\cancel{x}}-\\cancel{x}\\cdot \\frac{1-x}{\\cancel{x}} & =4x \\\\ x+7-(1-x) & =4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $x,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+7-(1-x) & =4x \\\\ 2x+6 & =4x \\\\ 4x-2x & =6 \\\\ 2x & =6 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $x=0$ as a solution. But since $x=3\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $x=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345135 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068880 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5916154-14b6-5e2d-b33c-176b76293927" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations Containing One Distinct Linear Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2685fdf5-b2ef-5419-a715-1c74ce9389ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342565} {:answer/id #uuid "26c73ff8-7bb1-58f5-b9d4-61756b11ddfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342568} {:answer/id #uuid "75251e7a-9236-5e4c-9b53-7cfd86320732" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343251} {:answer/id #uuid "978d082a-57ab-588e-8901-810af078103c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343568} {:answer/id #uuid "9f71a6a2-9c4d-582e-afe9-1afe12b68ba5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343645}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "26c73ff8-7bb1-58f5-b9d4-61756b11ddfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342568} :answer-field/id #uuid "4171e3f8-5f6b-53b2-9b4c-3f49129ae323" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344598}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dd8936d7-9acb-5f42-aac4-919b57567005" :question/math-academy-id "q-75925" :question/problem "Solve for $x$ where $\\frac{x-2}{5x}=\\frac{3-20x}{5x}+5.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that $x=0$ is an excluded solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nWe multiply both sides of the equation by $5x,$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-2}{5x} & =\\frac{3-20x}{5x}+5 \\\\ 5x\\cdot (\\frac{x-2}{5x}) & =5x\\cdot (\\frac{3-20x}{5x}+5) \\\\ 5x\\cdot \\frac{x-2}{5x} & =5x\\cdot \\frac{3-20x}{5x}+5x\\cdot 5 \\\\ \\cancel{5x}\\cdot \\frac{x-2}{\\cancel{5x}} & =\\cancel{5x}\\cdot \\frac{3-20x}{\\cancel{5x}}+25x \\\\ x-2 & =3-20x+25x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $x,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2 & =3-20x+25x \\\\ x-2 & =3+5x \\\\ 5x-x & =-2-3 \\\\ 4x & =-5 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $x=0$ as a solution. But since $x=-\\frac{5}{4}\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $x=-\\frac{5}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345137 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068882 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f63f80b8-f564-56e6-8c90-6918d64e0316" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations Containing Three Distinct Linear Denominators"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14a7452c-e3f9-526d-82be-805b605b288f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342420} {:answer/id #uuid "2377a0c3-c1bd-53c7-93bf-77cbc38fa76c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342532} {:answer/id #uuid "7c304e6a-4939-558c-9504-44c7f57d0bd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343307} {:answer/id #uuid "b825111c-c17e-5e28-b1d7-789b226b6fd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343852} {:answer/id #uuid "cef19a74-15fe-5db4-b7f1-7bf698a07ed9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344060}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b825111c-c17e-5e28-b1d7-789b226b6fd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343852} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c9331d1-6e4e-5569-b803-cfc624eda5cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344621}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "64167e5a-a3e2-55d1-956c-8945cc13aa71" :question/math-academy-id "q-75941" :question/problem "Which value of $m$ satisfies the equation $\\frac{2m-3}{3m}+\\frac{m+5}{5m}=\\frac{m+4}{15m}?$" :question/worked-solution "We note that $m=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators $3m,5m,$ and $15m.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(3m,5m,15m)=15m.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $15m,$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2m-3}{3m}+\\frac{m+5}{5m} & =\\frac{m+4}{15m} \\\\ 15m\\cdot (\\frac{2m-3}{3m}+\\frac{m+5}{5m}) & =15m\\cdot (\\frac{m+4}{15m}) \\\\ 15m\\cdot (\\frac{2m-3}{3m})+15m\\cdot (\\frac{m+5}{5m}) & =15m\\cdot (\\frac{m+4}{15m}) \\\\ 5\\cdot 3m\\cdot (\\frac{2m-3}{3m})+3\\cdot 5m\\cdot (\\frac{m+5}{5m}) & =15m\\cdot (\\frac{m+4}{15m}) \\\\ 5\\cdot \\cancel{3m}\\cdot (\\frac{2m-3}{\\cancel{3m}})+3\\cdot \\cancel{5m}\\cdot (\\frac{m+5}{\\cancel{5m}}) & =\\cancel{15m}\\cdot (\\frac{m+4}{\\cancel{15m}}) \\\\ 5(2m-3)+3(m+5) & =m+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $m,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(2m-3)+3(m+5) & =m+4 \\\\ 10m-15+3m+15 & =m+4 \\\\ 13m & =m+4 \\\\ 12m & =4 \\\\ m & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $m=0$ as a solution. But since $m=\\frac{1}{3}\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $m=\\frac{1}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396120 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087841 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e43e393-8752-58bc-b87c-b72f679cbfd9" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Lengths of the Major and Minor Axes Given the Equation of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2368068-c473-5919-aff6-53a14b246b5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6\\sqrt{2}$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396122} {:answer/id #uuid "801b88de-1112-5c33-bd1b-ddb864de2c6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3\\sqrt{2}$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396123} {:answer/id #uuid "dcd49079-798b-58f7-8490-cd6fb613a033" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396124} {:answer/id #uuid "d5b72ba2-9df1-5acc-b385-173c4e36f9a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$18$ and $9$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396125} {:answer/id #uuid "7c6ef04e-258f-5d68-a139-8ed8a39c2ddc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6\\sqrt{2}$ and $6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396126}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c6ef04e-258f-5d68-a139-8ed8a39c2ddc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6\\sqrt{2}$ and $6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396126} :answer-field/id #uuid "bef4ba21-7e9c-5949-8c09-0a45d37f8298" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396121}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "480a616c-8c80-5f81-b3d5-aa7488c0665b" :question/math-academy-id "q-7596" :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{18}=1?$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=9$ and ${b}^{2}=18.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=9,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{9}=3.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=18,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{18}=3\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $b=3\\sqrt{2},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=3.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 3\\sqrt{2}=6\\sqrt{2},$ and the minor axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 3=6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345144 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100918 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "51d50237-f6cf-5c2d-b8d1-335378205a28" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Convergence of Sequences Containing Exponential Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1caded66-c544-50e4-92e7-ee68a0ea1e1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342480} {:answer/id #uuid "4a78637a-f1b3-54af-bede-ea45a41273cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342850} {:answer/id #uuid "85bacb3b-5422-572c-8abd-1f411ccc1aa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The sequence diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343384} {:answer/id #uuid "985ece06-b158-505f-bbf1-5db5ef384f59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343575} {:answer/id #uuid "efb241ee-41d8-5ae3-b24b-55570171313d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{{e}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344344}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1caded66-c544-50e4-92e7-ee68a0ea1e1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342480} :answer-field/id #uuid "3233d4f6-e0f4-5864-aa06-929e37d35a50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344581}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c7877136-a6fe-51f9-a9fa-720076528d75" :question/math-academy-id "q-75962" :question/problem "What is the limit of the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡n}{{e}^{3n}}$ for $n\\ge 1?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nTo find the limit, we calculate\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡n}{{e}^{3n}}.\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡n}{{e}^{3n}}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence converges, and its limit is $0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345148 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068751 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5dff5b7c-544a-5fda-a175-51ce4723a135" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a \"Greater Than or Equal To\" Inequality Given the Graph of the Corresponding Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c23ac4e-07ed-5287-bb01-91aa2902b8af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x\\le -4$ or $x\\ge 2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342346} {:answer/id #uuid "24fe4290-5f4c-5d87-a699-c9ce2420d3a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x\\le -2$ or $x\\ge 4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342550} {:answer/id #uuid "83deac41-3d3c-5e2a-bfb6-0d7595c242b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\le x\\le 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343364} {:answer/id #uuid "baecd463-bb65-5296-8ffc-a008911a9131" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\le x\\le 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343881} {:answer/id #uuid "ed0dc6bb-6288-5e5f-996c-20637a73d576" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0\\le x\\le 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344321}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "baecd463-bb65-5296-8ffc-a008911a9131" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\le x\\le 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343881} :answer-field/id #uuid "e82fba37-8632-5ad8-8770-77f321f5bed3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344898}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6f1a6ebf-461a-5edb-8189-a41795a3f536" :question/math-academy-id "q-75976" :question/problem "![](images/f7/f74ae80071413963d1b71f91b99ae625edf6e285bb068be7b969483672f1288e.png)\n\nGiven the graph above, solve the inequality $-(x+2)(x-4)\\ge 0.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our inequality\n\n$$\n-(x+2)(x-4)\\ge 0,\n$$\n\nis a \"*greater than or equal to*\" inequality. Therefore, we need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola \n\n$$\ny=-(x+2)(x-4)\n$$\n\nis *above* the $x$-axis or *touches* this axis. We mark these values of $x$ in blue in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/2b/2bcb7336f352b1730a9c5b52060a4d49e9209cac73ca2688717f68c1af73168b.png)\n\nSo, the solution to the inequality is $-2\\le x\\le 4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064954 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064254 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b2a80aa-7882-50eb-bfe0-c0eeab72161d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Derivative Using Properties of Inverse Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "268c70ca-9195-5ed7-a069-2dddcc29c527" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342566} {:answer/id #uuid "787993cd-3298-5ce6-858f-c0975144378a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343279} {:answer/id #uuid "85f9d78e-b30c-534e-a8ad-42ebe5677c35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343389} {:answer/id #uuid "da79b542-167d-552a-a5e9-3a6c9ce02cd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344158} {:answer/id #uuid "f65c1423-e0a4-5ddf-a34e-98c6633665e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344406}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85f9d78e-b30c-534e-a8ad-42ebe5677c35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343389} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b976e39-0610-5ce5-af13-9d1cc55b54c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344680}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3dd725b3-7f44-5fca-9ff0-b43869ecbad5" :question/math-academy-id "q-75986" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-4).$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(0).$" :question/worked-solution "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(0),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(0)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(0).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(0)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(0) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =0 \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-4) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe discard $x=0$ since the logarithm is not defined here. Therefore, ${f}^{-1}(0)=5.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(5),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-4) \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-4)+\\frac{x}{x-4} \\\\ {f}^{′}(5) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5-4)+\\frac{5}{5-4} \\\\ {f}^{′}(5) & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345153 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064254 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b2a80aa-7882-50eb-bfe0-c0eeab72161d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Derivative Using Properties of Inverse Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c192211-a062-5d76-8e3f-cac4eb8e50eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342476} {:answer/id #uuid "2b04914a-76f1-51a8-a7c9-b2a4d26db2e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342597} {:answer/id #uuid "b72eebd1-ed24-5a3c-9407-3e4f89404335" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343845} {:answer/id #uuid "cd05bdbf-6440-5186-86f9-6868a4471153" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344045} {:answer/id #uuid "d38031bb-76d7-5bef-b74e-24bfabbc3646" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344094}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd05bdbf-6440-5186-86f9-6868a4471153" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344045} :answer-field/id #uuid "96deaadc-3e11-5c45-b3ef-17e9f80f5c36" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344754}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7a0673ad-ec47-547a-a169-9262732d993a" :question/math-academy-id "q-75987" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{2x+1}{x+1}.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(4).$" :question/worked-solution "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(4),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(4)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(4))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(4).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(4)=x.$ For $x\\ne -1,$ this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(4) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =4 \\\\ \\frac{2x+1}{x+1} & =4 \\\\ \\frac{2x+1}{x+1}\\cdot (x+1) & =4\\cdot (x+1) \\\\ \\frac{2x+1}{\\cancel{(x+1)}}\\cdot \\cancel{(x+1)} & =4x+4 \\\\ 2x+1 & =4x+4 \\\\ 2x & =-3 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{-1}(4)=-\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\nNow we calculate ${f}^{′}(-\\frac{3}{2}),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2x+1}{x+1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(-\\frac{3}{2}) & =\\frac{1}{{(-\\frac{3}{2}+1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(4) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(4))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(-\\frac{3}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345156 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184332 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef5d4f19-5602-54a9-96ea-4e42054ff5c8" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Greater Than (Or Equal To) Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01b60310-2754-5cd5-aa34-2bfdde897eaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342264} {:answer/id #uuid "547c2421-5f8e-5902-b77f-7b6f15ff9451" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3<x<5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342940} {:answer/id #uuid "cdb5c572-1d47-5d01-bfe0-520955fba02e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x<-3$ or $x>5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344053} {:answer/id #uuid "e475c534-5b69-5639-a86d-516483dcde38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5<x<3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344243} {:answer/id #uuid "fa353900-d64b-519f-8e03-9312e2b16455" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x<-5$ or $x>3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344438}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e475c534-5b69-5639-a86d-516483dcde38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5<x<3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344243} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7f311ba-13e5-5ab9-b5f3-f892490ae0bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344817}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ca52724e-95d0-555c-b582-e5e500954e40" :question/math-academy-id "q-75992" :question/problem "Find the solution of $-{x}^{2}-2x+15>0.$" :question/worked-solution "To solve the inequality \n\n$$\n-{x}^{2}-2x+15>0,\n$$\n\nwe need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola \n\n$$\ny=-{x}^{2}-2x+15\n$$\n\nlies *above* the $x$-axis.\n\nFirst, let's find the roots of the parabola:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}-2x+15 & =0 \\\\ -({x}^{2}+2x-15) & =0 \\\\ -(x+5)(x-3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the roots are $x=-5$ and $x=3,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](images/da/da88a1971a7a389c8f5b59ebd3cef2a146eb09113c3e71031bf9dfb6cdbe91e1.png)\n\nThe values marked in blue show all $x$-values where the parabola lies *above* the axis. \n\nSo, the solution to the inequality is $-5<x<3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393570 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152167 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2b677c92-5390-5cd1-b1d3-b84d180c2b3e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Complex Conjugate of a Complex Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b105609a-626e-5869-b128-d197a399d5d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-9+6\\text{i} \\\\ -9+3\\text{i} \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393572} {:answer/id #uuid "3de2a71d-db0b-5939-8da1-7ca8be090ca6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6+9\\text{i} \\\\ 3+9\\text{i} \\\\ -3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393573} {:answer/id #uuid "9b6cbc9a-09ac-5215-8c7d-54852633b7ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6-9\\text{i} \\\\ 3-9\\text{i} \\\\ -3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393574} {:answer/id #uuid "93b794e4-166e-521c-b5c2-17eea946b685" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9+6\\text{i} \\\\ 9+3\\text{i} \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393575} {:answer/id #uuid "90238b0d-fa91-54f4-a553-5d13bdfba4ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-9-6\\text{i} \\\\ -9-3\\text{i} \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393576}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93b794e4-166e-521c-b5c2-17eea946b685" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9+6\\text{i} \\\\ 9+3\\text{i} \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393575} :answer-field/id #uuid "271612bf-2589-524c-8953-53d9f15d0df7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393571}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4302a7c6-021d-538a-9010-38418efea819" :question/math-academy-id "q-76039" :question/problem "If $\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}9-6\\text{i} \\\\ 9-3\\text{i} \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\,$ then $\\,\\overset{―}{x}=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the complex conjugate of a vector, we take the complex conjugate of the entries of the vector. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\overset{―}{[\\begin{aligned}9-6\\text{i} \\\\ 9-3\\text{i} \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{9-6\\text{i}} \\\\ \\overset{―}{9-3\\text{i}} \\\\ \\overset{―}{-3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9+6\\text{i} \\\\ 9+3\\text{i} \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345163 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152168 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "689f9f44-4776-56a7-915f-d0dab0755002" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Components of the Real and Imaginary Parts of a Complex Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4bbb921d-f6f4-5480-bfbf-c6d90fe64647" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342862} {:answer/id #uuid "50b5dc8d-7e77-5191-97c8-29fe680f3d1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342906} {:answer/id #uuid "92681936-d9cd-5044-b0fa-03426cefa6cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343516} {:answer/id #uuid "d5e79586-dfc4-5a38-b455-7f21a76e52eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "43" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344110} {:answer/id #uuid "f73494d8-9f5d-5cac-850f-97428a0e3f15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344415}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d5e79586-dfc4-5a38-b455-7f21a76e52eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "43" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344110} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f2ed79a-afc0-55dd-b367-a5beca302e47" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344693}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "092a46e1-ab30-51e7-8a06-0f91ed1c7242" :question/math-academy-id "q-76050" :question/problem "If $\\,\\text{Re}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\text{Im}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}c \\\\ d\\end{aligned}]\\,$ are the real and imaginary parts of the vector $\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}-6+7\\text{i} \\\\ 1-6\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]\\,$ then what is the value of $ad+bc?$" :question/worked-solution "For the given vector $\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}-6+7\\text{i} \\\\ 1-6\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]\\,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =[\\begin{aligned}-6+7\\text{i} \\\\ 1-6\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(x)}+\\text{i}\\,{{[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore $\\,a=-6,b=1,c=7$ and $d=-6$, which implies that \n\n$$\nad+bc=(-6)\\cdot (-6)+1\\cdot 7=43.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345168 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129157 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c7c1b7ad-e4bf-55b2-8665-2cf35e402946" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Complex Conjugate of a Complex Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41dd09f6-0939-5506-a351-fbad720a41e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1+7\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342782} {:answer/id #uuid "624ba52f-cb14-5738-9454-669c81055e83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-7\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343073} {:answer/id #uuid "63b53a99-7c78-5500-9ec4-14d6307bcd52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7+\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343083} {:answer/id #uuid "72f83b85-6723-5210-94da-dbd63f781e20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7+\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343234} {:answer/id #uuid "a5f74711-7d96-5f76-9b61-e8b9c67d7e37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7-\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343699}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63b53a99-7c78-5500-9ec4-14d6307bcd52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7+\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343083} :answer-field/id #uuid "4dd3289e-d5fa-50f8-884e-179d97dc6e97" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344625}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "aad215f0-b1cf-5385-a89c-46aa554c6d26" :question/math-academy-id "q-76057" :question/problem "Find $B=\\overset{―}{A}$, where $A=[\\begin{aligned}9-7\\text{i} & -2\\text{i} & 6-\\text{i} \\\\ -7 & 5-\\text{i} & \\text{i} \\\\ 6 & -\\text{i} & 0\\end{aligned}].$ What is the value of ${b}_{21}+{b}_{32}?$" :question/worked-solution "Taking the complex conjugate of each entry, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{A} & =\\overset{―}{[\\begin{aligned}9-7\\text{i} & -2\\text{i} & 6-\\text{i} \\\\ -7 & 5-\\text{i} & \\text{i} \\\\ 6 & -\\text{i} & 0\\end{aligned}]} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{9-7\\text{i}} & \\overset{―}{-2\\text{i}} & \\overset{―}{6-\\text{i}} \\\\ \\overset{―}{-7} & \\overset{―}{5-\\text{i}} & \\overset{―}{\\text{i}} \\\\ \\overset{―}{6} & \\overset{―}{-\\text{i}} & \\overset{―}{0}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9+7\\text{i} & 2\\text{i} & 6+\\text{i} \\\\ -7 & 5+\\text{i} & -\\text{i} \\\\ 6 & \\text{i} & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${b}_{21}+{b}_{32}=-7+\\text{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345173 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129093 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "195aa6d7-fbb5-5b8f-8c0f-0df7a844ad59" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intervals on Which a Radical Function Is Continuous"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1426a86e-4086-5ced-98c6-c90cadebf9f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-6,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342416} {:answer/id #uuid "1d04513d-c428-580d-ba8c-fdf130c84af0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-6,-2]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342481} {:answer/id #uuid "716ad020-ec8f-540d-ac8b-a63ee165bd63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343214} {:answer/id #uuid "771f2f12-cc2a-5abc-a5a9-62f29304dea4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-6,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343271} {:answer/id #uuid "d17ebd2d-1493-5f74-8387-77d402809ba2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344079}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "716ad020-ec8f-540d-ac8b-a63ee165bd63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343214} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1e39a4e-3bac-5aa6-93a8-704d5cedd11c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344855}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a4448830-b102-5b0e-8785-87819bbce0fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-76067" :question/problem "Determine the largest interval on which the function $f(x)=\\sqrt{6+x}+\\sqrt{5x+10}$ is continuous." :question/worked-solution "Our function consists of two radical functions, so it is continuous over all points in its domain.\n\n- To find the domain of $\\sqrt{6+x}$, we set the expression under the radical to be greater than or equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6+x\\ge 0\\,⟹\\,x\\ge -6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find the domain of $\\sqrt{5x+10}$, we set the expression under the radical to be greater than or equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+10\\ge 0\\,⟹\\,5x\\ge -10\\,⟹\\,x\\ge -2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe domain of $f(x)$ is given by the intersection of the domain of our radical functions:\n\n$$\n[-6,\\infty )\\cap [-2,\\infty )=[-2,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nand the function is continuous on this interval."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345174 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129094 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eac988ba-ea97-52c7-90a7-d451ee7f49e5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intervals on Which a Rational Function Is Continuous"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c45702c-203b-5ff9-be02-36cd39cd3a12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342347} {:answer/id #uuid "3791bf26-4519-5472-92ad-31671cc7eb39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,4)\\cup (4,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342696} {:answer/id #uuid "69199960-23c6-55ba-8694-1568feb64f56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,2)\\cup (2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343136} {:answer/id #uuid "79b19ede-490c-5109-82d4-9423b7c6bb7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (-2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343286} {:answer/id #uuid "b9445c1c-b052-5bfe-9c1d-6721e8b051d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343867}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69199960-23c6-55ba-8694-1568feb64f56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,2)\\cup (2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343136} :answer-field/id #uuid "26398174-98d8-5aa8-8043-cb866796eb74" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344562}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "93c4f9ce-9d85-5806-8532-dd3e07cb9302" :question/math-academy-id "q-76069" :question/problem "Determine the largest union of intervals on which the function $f(x)=\\frac{x-2}{4{x}^{2}-16x+16}$ is continuous." :question/worked-solution "This is a rational function, so it is continuous at all points in its domain. To find its domain, we start by factoring the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x-2}{4{x}^{2}-16x+16} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-2}{4({x}^{2}-4x+4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-2}{4(x-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4(x-2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis function is continuous everywhere except at the excluded point $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, the domain consists of the union of the intervals $(-\\infty ,2)$ and $(2,\\infty ),$ namely\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,2)\\cup (2,\\infty )\n$$\n\nand the function is also continuous on these intervals."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345176 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129095 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0c9958ee-c8b0-5d33-8755-e61cb445b0de" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intervals on Which a Logarithmic Function Is Continuous"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41d37236-cdb6-53ee-ae81-dc11f5d0e79b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342779} {:answer/id #uuid "4dadc677-4324-5cdf-b675-729c97ce84df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342876} {:answer/id #uuid "5c70c532-84d5-5a36-84e2-40bd34dd4357" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-1]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343015} {:answer/id #uuid "83e928db-d13d-593e-8ae9-e081a49cb520" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343365} {:answer/id #uuid "c93916ab-a15a-5a14-a0a8-3bd86a3b0e4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344003}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c93916ab-a15a-5a14-a0a8-3bd86a3b0e4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344003} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c36ad8d-8507-5cc6-908d-82f87d6d13e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344520}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "39454c83-6327-5d97-8cd7-df542c8ec3d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-76071" :question/problem "Find the largest interval of continuity for the function $f(x)={log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{2x+2}{3+{2}^{x}}).$" :question/worked-solution "Our function is a logarithmic function, so it is continuous over all points in its domain.\n\nTo find the domain of ${log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{2x+2}{3+{2}^{x}}),$ we set the expression under the logarithm to be greater than zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+2}{3+{2}^{x}}>0\\,⟹\\,2x+2>0\\,⟹\\,x>-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $f(x)={log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{2x+2}{3+{2}^{x}})$ is $(-1,\\infty ),$ and it is also continuous on this interval."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345178 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087840 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "de557d1d-5f27-50e3-bcba-22b21f83267f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Tall Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20a06fb7-fb76-56d8-b6e1-f809612a62d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342506} {:answer/id #uuid "25b26601-0bd0-5ba9-8edc-93a16701e235" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{5}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342556} {:answer/id #uuid "325931bd-edd2-5aee-b4bb-db09428a96e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{5}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342663} {:answer/id #uuid "d456c171-7bf0-551c-826b-9b80db67e5fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{18}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344101} {:answer/id #uuid "f9b91c50-fe18-5dce-ac87-c7a197511c9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344434}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20a06fb7-fb76-56d8-b6e1-f809612a62d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342506} :answer-field/id #uuid "ffbc37fc-8913-56b6-aa24-4d3f0156cd73" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344943}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "efae30e0-3c67-576a-b125-57ed6ed688b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-7608" :question/problem "![](images/20/202dec2a2e75aa6720181eb925f98999031453f8f1f8822dd86d7a2baab21396.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=3$ and the vertical radius has length $b=5.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393577 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078404 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e806aac-62fc-5c53-a15e-a2ce175dad25" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Open Intervals of Continuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "506667f4-1745-5e4d-85a9-7600caf82729" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/27/27cc3b76baa60c48f103f7d016bb78716330dbb03be791458489e5458bf326bd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393579} {:answer/id #uuid "0ba3e508-a2c3-510a-a873-37949da99395" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c5/c56ea94b0d5102c276d7cd4a196517a41611e3f7da82b3ab13c6b404c31cf430.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393580} {:answer/id #uuid "8cfe7aac-2e56-5220-a210-68874685d980" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/59/59bc174fabd3f93321505a54f05832fda46914c8f8f8ad5e88690f53bef3e845.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393581} {:answer/id #uuid "dde21ed5-68b8-5e4d-8274-2b62e50ca71a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/47/47dca98b1ac1c7d3310418c3d5eaf58416827aaa5d97d118aa5f3b4a6d59d9f8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393582} {:answer/id #uuid "daafc43e-468c-51ed-8556-5bcac5a8828f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c6/c6cfdc41036d1dc319054c0cf397276f40faa2c749ea59820b45875cb31b3d9f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393583}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "daafc43e-468c-51ed-8556-5bcac5a8828f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c6/c6cfdc41036d1dc319054c0cf397276f40faa2c749ea59820b45875cb31b3d9f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393583} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d67e4e9-9e71-5de1-b090-03f1f092f56a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393578}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b8055c33-18c1-54b4-a55d-b97e3d2f96f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-76091" :question/problem "Which of the following functions is continuous on the interval $(-2,\\infty )?$ You may assume that all points of discontinuity for each function are shown." :question/worked-solution "The correct answer is the following.\n\n  \n\n![](images/c6/c6cfdc41036d1dc319054c0cf397276f40faa2c749ea59820b45875cb31b3d9f.png)\n\n    \n\nIndeed, we have that $h(x)$ is continuous on the open interval $(-2,\\infty ).$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=0,$ but this point lies within the interval $(-2,\\infty ),$\n\n- $q(x)$ and $p(x)$ are not continuous at $x=1,$ but this point lies within the interval $(-2,\\infty ),$\n\n- $g(x)$is not continuous at $x=2,$ but this point lies within the interval $(-2,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345179 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064254 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b2a80aa-7882-50eb-bfe0-c0eeab72161d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Derivative Using Properties of Inverse Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a8011c1-9cfc-5fa1-bbdd-091c67e18332" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342719} {:answer/id #uuid "63cef5b5-d373-5b1f-8528-cc13e3026246" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343086} {:answer/id #uuid "6cc3db4c-1ba1-5e07-a179-70559ebd7afe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343169} {:answer/id #uuid "725e4d8f-30f3-541a-b361-47b9d40fc3e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343229} {:answer/id #uuid "d7be1e3b-a646-5934-9f54-a7f5ec33461f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344133}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "725e4d8f-30f3-541a-b361-47b9d40fc3e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343229} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b10f5e9-21ca-540c-a98f-057955c52343" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344677}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9c47b28a-2993-50a5-9de3-ebf5581e87c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-76106" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\sqrt{2x-1}.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(3).$" :question/worked-solution "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(3),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(3)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(3))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(3).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(3)=x.$ For $x>\\frac{1}{2}$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(3) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =3 \\\\ \\sqrt{2x-1} & =3 \\\\ 2x-1 & =9 \\\\ 2x & =10 \\\\ x & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(5),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\sqrt{2x-1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x-1}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(5) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\cdot 5-1}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(5) & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(3) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(3))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{3})} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366274 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064254 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b2a80aa-7882-50eb-bfe0-c0eeab72161d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Derivative Using Properties of Inverse Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b42161f-1560-5a88-b672-16d3a6c105b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366276} {:answer/id #uuid "b9c48b02-063f-5744-be52-d2ccdaf85fa9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366277} {:answer/id #uuid "221a20c8-4569-5827-b5a3-0104b98273d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366278} {:answer/id #uuid "96652cb2-c644-5003-8a9d-a90f516c4ec0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{32\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366279} {:answer/id #uuid "590cda43-c9f1-5ada-b5d9-d47306c771f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366280}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "221a20c8-4569-5827-b5a3-0104b98273d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366278} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5390472-80eb-5d87-8281-9e88c9d4585c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366275}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "67a843ac-f40c-59da-8377-455b0890a108" :question/math-academy-id "q-76108" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)={2}^{x}+1.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(5).$" :question/worked-solution "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(5),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(5)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(5))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(5).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(5)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(5) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =5 \\\\ {2}^{x}+1 & =5 \\\\ {2}^{x} & =4 \\\\ {2}^{x} & ={2}^{2} \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{-1}(5)=2.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={2}^{x}+1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot {2}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(5) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(5))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399050 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078404 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e806aac-62fc-5c53-a15e-a2ce175dad25" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Open Intervals of Continuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7db323bd-027d-5a72-8744-915dc9466d84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399052} {:answer/id #uuid "363b9e46-0e55-5119-8918-a99ef9c17cd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399053} {:answer/id #uuid "c64155b0-249e-5565-ad41-5aa4d4de25a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399054} {:answer/id #uuid "ab114369-abaf-5996-96e1-bc98ccf909b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399055} {:answer/id #uuid "5bd9f381-647a-53f8-8916-ebcaa02fb734" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399056}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c64155b0-249e-5565-ad41-5aa4d4de25a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399054} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad20513b-7f6b-51c1-b3e8-54f31e6cfac0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399051}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bfd92570-bd08-5565-9c64-935de2ba2369" :question/math-academy-id "q-76110" :question/problem "![](images/76/760ddc657e8372a3860e2553e4f2b713a8b12544586c708e5f2c1922462567a8.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $y=f(x)$ above continuous? You may assume that all points of discontinuity of $f(x)$ are shown in the diagram." :question/worked-solution "To determine the intervals of continuity of a function, we take the domain of the function and exclude any points of discontinuity.\n\nThe function is defined on the intervals $(-\\infty ,3),$  and $(3,\\infty ).$ There is a discontinuity at $x=3$  but this is already excluded.  \n\nThere is also a discontinuity at $x=-3,$ so we need to exclude that.\n\nTherefore, the function is continuous on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-3),$ $(-3,3),$  and $(3,\\infty ).$ It is also continuous on any subinterval of these intervals. \n\nAmong the answer choices, the correct one is the following:\n\n$\\,$ $(-2,3)$ \n\nNotice that:\n\n- $(-3,4)$ and $(0,5)$ contain the excluded point $x=3,$ so the function is not continuous on these intervals.\n\n- $(-\\infty ,-2)$ and  $(-\\infty ,0)$ contain the point of discontinuity $x=-3$, so the function is not continuous on these intervals."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064955 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064254 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b2a80aa-7882-50eb-bfe0-c0eeab72161d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Derivative Using Properties of Inverse Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2582e4d6-6e26-52cf-b655-68828c0dd1ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342553} {:answer/id #uuid "6b269a15-8d61-520d-b043-65a4e93a81ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343152} {:answer/id #uuid "8984d421-f5ec-519c-943e-f33baf6c2e58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343432} {:answer/id #uuid "a7a064a2-438a-58c7-bd56-2d31409767e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343714} {:answer/id #uuid "ebff3471-8046-54fc-b532-7ca6873c0568" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344307}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ebff3471-8046-54fc-b532-7ca6873c0568" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344307} :answer-field/id #uuid "2299e478-f0ab-52b2-8744-b6514e20fbc9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344555}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4a674864-526f-5399-9fef-530d9892c47d" :question/math-academy-id "q-76114" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{3}+2x+1.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(1).$" :question/worked-solution "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(1),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(1).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(1)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(1) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =1 \\\\ {x}^{3}+2x+1 & =1 \\\\ x({x}^{2}+2) & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{-1}(1)=0.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(0),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{3}+2x+1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}+2 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =3\\cdot 0+2 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345188 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087833 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "57e19ee3-db9a-58d7-8ca3-4e8801aee656" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Tall Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ee3d757-9c11-529a-853f-800224ed53f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{9}+(y-2)^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342631} {:answer/id #uuid "4af06850-a9e7-5958-87c8-eca061d4a95b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+2)^{2}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{3}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342855} {:answer/id #uuid "7f56ea67-57ce-5a9c-afd1-b8cf934f86a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+2)^{2}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{9}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343332} {:answer/id #uuid "a8995e69-24a2-5f25-9f1f-f6152bb8456c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{9}+(y+2)^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343723} {:answer/id #uuid "d265a133-79db-517a-af76-0eeaff4e4bf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}+\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{3}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344082}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f56ea67-57ce-5a9c-afd1-b8cf934f86a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+2)^{2}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{9}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343332} :answer-field/id #uuid "f297f988-c765-53b7-92f6-ff0a624bb3de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344915}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "25c67443-abb8-58a6-bbc2-b7f961d0e0ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-7619" :question/problem "![](images/2b/2bc4b82c6cfae63026f221dd49cdc74a9ce3884443e933cae02c36f6d732707e.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the ellipse is centered at $(-2,4),$ the horizontal radius has length $a=1,$ and the vertical radius has length $b=3.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-(-2))^{2}}{{1}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ (x+2)^{2}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{9} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345191 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129132 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c28b8ae-71ef-5d26-895a-5d949fc5b164" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Components of the Real and Imaginary Parts of a Complex Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "504d2b32-4184-5dcd-becb-dd145229ab0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342903} {:answer/id #uuid "78f37c8f-5e5d-5a35-9335-be78340c647c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343284} {:answer/id #uuid "90cadb7e-a939-5cc2-8497-018dc70ed109" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343504} {:answer/id #uuid "b7027eac-2319-52a1-84f4-d072b9f1e5ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343842} {:answer/id #uuid "f6ed83df-6778-53c1-9ba8-984f5b6fd37d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344412}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "90cadb7e-a939-5cc2-8497-018dc70ed109" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343504} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea07266e-71f4-5499-afbc-3b0c53da3e79" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344899}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ec506ad6-de50-599d-8a2a-07eff3ab4556" :question/math-academy-id "q-76203" :question/problem "If $\\,\\text{Re}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\text{Im}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}e & f \\\\ g & h\\end{aligned}]\\,$ are the real and imaginary parts of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-3\\text{i} & 5\\text{i} \\\\ -4 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\,$ then what is the value of $ah-cf?$" :question/worked-solution "For the given matrix $A,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-3\\text{i} & 5\\text{i} \\\\ -4 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0-3\\text{i} & 0+5\\text{i} \\\\ -4+0\\text{i} & 5+0\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(A)}+\\text{i}{{[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(A)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $a=0,c=-4,f=5$ and $h=0.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nah-cf=0\\cdot 0-(-4)\\cdot 5=20.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393584 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129132 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c28b8ae-71ef-5d26-895a-5d949fc5b164" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Components of the Real and Imaginary Parts of a Complex Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e5c72b2-290f-5867-aff1-f44b222e372d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 7 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393586} {:answer/id #uuid "92b33271-0fa4-553d-a1ab-66985b701f62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393587} {:answer/id #uuid "01d9e892-9ecc-5b10-9511-71135ba52589" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393588} {:answer/id #uuid "48bb8ceb-d5f2-5ad5-bd55-119d025e8ba6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393589} {:answer/id #uuid "466efbf9-c64b-5ebc-b990-de2ab1f13120" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393590}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01d9e892-9ecc-5b10-9511-71135ba52589" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393588} :answer-field/id #uuid "5daf3dd3-3305-5d23-9005-c484c6593e98" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393585}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "58793c96-ddf8-519d-986f-39d6955284e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-76204" :question/problem "Find the real part of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1+2\\text{i} & 7-6\\text{i} \\\\ -\\text{i} & 4\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "For the given matrix $A,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1+2\\text{i} & 7-6\\text{i} \\\\ -\\text{i} & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1+2\\text{i} & 7-6\\text{i} \\\\ 0-\\text{i} & 4+0\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(A)}+\\text{i}{{[\\begin{aligned}2 & -6 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(A)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{Re}(A)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345192 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129132 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c28b8ae-71ef-5d26-895a-5d949fc5b164" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Components of the Real and Imaginary Parts of a Complex Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41d289b1-173b-5941-a82a-cd3eb177ca1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342778} {:answer/id #uuid "5590b136-2148-5765-ba42-4872df4f3a5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342949} {:answer/id #uuid "b7b63991-e097-507e-8d58-bb1825341d01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343849} {:answer/id #uuid "bddcd176-e192-5da2-98c9-8a41a86d4f96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343909} {:answer/id #uuid "c13c6f25-efb1-5263-8ada-3dc3f47c23f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ 9 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343935}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41d289b1-173b-5941-a82a-cd3eb177ca1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342778} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4ad0e08-50c7-5ef0-a697-128f90e1eea2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344921}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ee75bb73-f205-5858-a7b6-5d0fd3c5399e" :question/math-academy-id "q-76207" :question/problem "Find the imaginary part of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}8+\\text{i} & 3-4\\text{i} \\\\ 9 & \\text{i}\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "For the given matrix $A,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}8+\\text{i} & 3-4\\text{i} \\\\ 9 & \\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8+\\text{i} & 3-4\\text{i} \\\\ 9+0\\text{i} & 0+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}8 & 3 \\\\ 9 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(A)}+\\text{i}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(A)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{Im}(A)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393598 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129156 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "83fad21d-6b36-5198-a13f-362d34342475" :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying Complex Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ddfb309-03f1-5086-b134-9a300a1e4991" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5+9\\text{i} & 9-13\\text{i} \\\\ 10+5\\text{i} & 14-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393600} {:answer/id #uuid "fde0ddb4-cac3-5fbb-b0e8-ee77e56d4d13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}10+5\\text{i} & 14-3\\text{i} \\\\ -5+9\\text{i} & 9-13\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393601} {:answer/id #uuid "565144e9-65e3-5c8e-b095-b218ef949235" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5+9\\text{i} & 14-3\\text{i} \\\\ 10+5\\text{i} & 9-13\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393602} {:answer/id #uuid "d3ca5b76-d9a3-5bd3-96bd-948909e64409" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}14-3\\text{i} & 9-13\\text{i} \\\\ -5+9\\text{i} & 10+5\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393603} {:answer/id #uuid "73837f27-75b0-552d-920f-58cabd54c6a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}10+5\\text{i} & 9-13\\text{i} \\\\ -5+9\\text{i} & 14-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393604}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "73837f27-75b0-552d-920f-58cabd54c6a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}10+5\\text{i} & 9-13\\text{i} \\\\ -5+9\\text{i} & 14-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393604} :answer-field/id #uuid "416179a7-3219-5ce9-9303-152f6654f152" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393599}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eaa32e2e-c807-5da3-b10a-a7704147713e" :question/math-academy-id "q-76217" :question/problem "If $\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}2-3\\text{i} & \\text{i} \\\\ 3 & -1-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}2\\text{i} & 5 \\\\ 1-4\\text{i} & 2+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]\\,$, then $AB=$" :question/worked-solution "Multiplying the matrices using the usual method, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}2-3\\text{i} & \\text{i} \\\\ 3 & -1-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2\\text{i} & 5 \\\\ 1-4\\text{i} & 2+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(2-3\\text{i})\\cdot 2\\text{i}+\\text{i}\\cdot (1-4\\text{i}) & (2-3\\text{i})\\cdot 5+\\text{i}\\cdot (2+\\text{i}) \\\\ 3\\cdot 2\\text{i}+(-1-\\text{i})(1-4\\text{i}) & 3\\cdot 5+(-1-\\text{i})(2+\\text{i})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(4\\text{i}+6)+(\\text{i}+4) & (10-15\\text{i})+(2\\text{i}-1) \\\\ 6\\text{i}+(-5+3\\text{i}) & 15+(-1-3\\text{i})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10+5\\text{i} & 9-13\\text{i} \\\\ -5+9\\text{i} & 14-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345202 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129133 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef2e7753-83f4-5826-a04f-89616d34c578" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Row Operations to a Complex Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1805badb-f754-528a-8d52-c015c7fc5279" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} & \\text{i} \\\\ 1 & 8\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342451} {:answer/id #uuid "5478876e-b8dc-51e1-a29d-6f7a3d56fccb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} & \\text{i} \\\\ 0 & 8\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342939} {:answer/id #uuid "7bbdd958-9845-5cff-960d-97997a9275f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} & \\text{i} \\\\ 1 & 10-2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343303} {:answer/id #uuid "ec3cb9b0-0735-586c-9b2d-809f38fcce02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} & \\text{i} \\\\ 0 & 10-2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344310} {:answer/id #uuid "f22dbc4e-5306-5a5f-baae-0f748bc71a57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344368}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec3cb9b0-0735-586c-9b2d-809f38fcce02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} & \\text{i} \\\\ 0 & 10-2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344310} :answer-field/id #uuid "50b4245b-055e-5a51-b938-d6e9856171ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344631}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a88b6c4c-7d86-59b8-a20e-ef073aed49d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-76254" :question/problem "Given the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} & \\text{i} \\\\ 7 & 3-2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$, find the result of applying to $A$ the following elementary row operations: \n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{7}{\\text{i}}{R}_{1}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the row operations to $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} & \\text{i} \\\\ 7 & 3-2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{7}{\\text{i}}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} & \\text{i} \\\\ 7+(-\\text{i})\\cdot \\frac{7}{\\text{i}} & \\text{(}3-2\\text{i})+i\\cdot \\frac{7}{\\text{i}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} & \\text{i} \\\\ 0 & 10-2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the result  is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} & \\text{i} \\\\ 0 & 10-2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088329 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087833 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "57e19ee3-db9a-58d7-8ca3-4e8801aee656" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Tall Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22cca71a-f877-530d-bdb9-a213241318c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{16}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342526} {:answer/id #uuid "4f116b80-2aa1-5c59-8cd2-80b4b8c8de09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{8}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342889} {:answer/id #uuid "b9fc9f14-0afd-5d7f-923d-5bc14fa19d95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{8}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343871} {:answer/id #uuid "d9804271-d0b8-536e-9b4c-ecaddb9a5410" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{16}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344146} {:answer/id #uuid "ea2c1000-b8ea-5681-b70f-ab99131b5478" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{16}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344288}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22cca71a-f877-530d-bdb9-a213241318c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{16}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342526} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a41b7bb-e834-5f07-bcf0-1ce27db07919" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344759}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "573849bd-9036-5460-9507-bc3e024db188" :question/math-academy-id "q-7627" :question/problem "![](images/2a/2ab9745cb9d157e566c101a6ca79514017903fa57eaf8725c846afd272c74f93.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the ellipse is centered at $(3,-1),$ the horizontal radius has length $a=2,$ and the vertical radius has length $b=4.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-(-1))^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{16} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345205 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087726 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d21d6066-f7c8-5a47-b150-d0ae25d2d60b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Center and Axis Lengths Given an Inhomogeneous Polynomial Equation of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3adfcb62-7ac7-590b-be06-2e8243f4dc65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342720} {:answer/id #uuid "4ad1531f-e1cf-5ebe-ad81-a38e2a113f1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342853} {:answer/id #uuid "6f8cbcdc-3ebb-5a0c-ab73-51b9ffaf200d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343197} {:answer/id #uuid "7e843273-2386-57fa-b165-5aee9ac338af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343328} {:answer/id #uuid "f2d228be-5c04-5444-b3a0-25193a937280" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344371}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2d228be-5c04-5444-b3a0-25193a937280" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344371} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab2f6a43-fe63-5c02-bab3-608eea1fae56" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344793}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b972a86c-4480-5828-a8e2-d62f9765dca2" :question/math-academy-id "q-7632" :question/problem "Determine the center of the ellipse whose equation is $5{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}-10x-36y=4.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must group the $x$ terms and the $y$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}-10x-36y & =4 \\\\ (5{x}^{2}-10x)+(9{y}^{2}-36y) & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must complete the square for both the $x$ and $y$ terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5{x}^{2}-10x)+(9{y}^{2}-36y) & =4 \\\\ 5[{x}^{2}-2x]+9[{y}^{2}-4y] & =4 \\\\ 5[(x-1)^{2}-{1}^{2}]+9[(y-2)^{2}-{2}^{2}] & =4 \\\\ 5[(x-1)^{2}-1]+9[(y-2)^{2}-4] & =4 \\\\ 5(x-1)^{2}-5+9(y-2)^{2}-36 & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rearrange the equation into the desired form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(x-1)^{2}-5+9(y-2)^{2}-36 & =4 \\\\ 5(x-1)^{2}+9(y-2)^{2} & =4+5+36 \\\\ 5(x-1)^{2}+9(y-2)^{2} & =45 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{5} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{(\\sqrt{5})^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse is centered at $(1,2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403836 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078391 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "98e3f3b4-8dfa-515c-90b1-09fb19d68144" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Center and Radius of a Circle by Completing the Square with Fractions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "707a4cfe-d34e-5500-a601-9753b554d97e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(1,-\\frac{1}{4}),$ radius: $\\frac{5}{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403838} {:answer/id #uuid "0043bbe1-8c13-5357-841d-e795a04df199" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(-1,-\\frac{1}{4}),$ radius: $\\frac{3}{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403839} {:answer/id #uuid "13f05de5-d7d8-5d9c-9e5d-ac75c76fa048" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(1,\\frac{1}{4}),$ radius: $\\frac{3}{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403840} {:answer/id #uuid "1705b14f-f5fa-5468-b93d-33bcdcdbb387" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(\\frac{1}{4},1),$ radius: $\\frac{7}{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403841} {:answer/id #uuid "cc1a32ca-3acb-5d7d-b8ad-8a49ebecf6c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(-1,\\frac{1}{4}),$ radius: $\\frac{5}{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403842}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13f05de5-d7d8-5d9c-9e5d-ac75c76fa048" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(1,\\frac{1}{4}),$ radius: $\\frac{3}{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403840} :answer-field/id #uuid "64251e03-03e9-594a-89a0-0a7ae7fb1367" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403837}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "64257e64-8ece-56eb-a9da-46dae15085ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-76374" :question/problem "Find the radius and center of the circle whose equation is\n$2{x}^{2}-4x+2{y}^{2}-y=-1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the center and radius of the circle, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-4x+2{y}^{2}-y & =-1 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+{y}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}y & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2x)+({y}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}y) & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2\\cdot x+{1}^{2})+({y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}y+{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}) & =-\\frac{1}{2}+{1}^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2} \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2\\cdot x+{1}^{2})+({y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}y+{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}) & =\\frac{9}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can factor the perfect squares and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2}+{(y-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2} & =\\frac{9}{16} \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+{(y-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2} & ={(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the circle is centered at $(1,\\frac{1}{4})$ and its radius is $\\frac{3}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345212 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078550 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "07a3dfec-3f7b-5820-9be5-12bf4acd5a3b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Term of an Arithmetic Series Given a Term and the Sum"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0205c844-2ff5-5390-b203-852d23c56776" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-68" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342267} {:answer/id #uuid "2da00fb3-57b5-580d-9f35-be3375459bc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "65" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342621} {:answer/id #uuid "6605ad56-9d95-513d-93bb-56bacaa33a22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "62" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343104} {:answer/id #uuid "be5219ae-5bc0-5b4c-b080-7eaaed722a4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "71" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343914} {:answer/id #uuid "e09d367f-7d39-514f-824c-6618c6b5c292" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-77" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344209}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2da00fb3-57b5-580d-9f35-be3375459bc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "65" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342621} :answer-field/id #uuid "6985455a-cb44-55a7-80ed-7751a1b3eca1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344674}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "63be1e3e-0c7e-532a-83da-c37dcd016580" :question/math-academy-id "q-76376" :question/problem "Find the first term of an arithmetic series if its $17$th term is $33$ and the sum of the first $30$ terms is $1\\,080.$" :question/worked-solution "We are given one term of the series. So, to find the first term, we use the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series in the form\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term, $d$ is the common difference, and $N$ is the number of terms.\n\nSubstituting $N=30$ and ${S}_{N}=1\\,080$ into the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\ 1\\,080 & =\\frac{30}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(30-1)d) \\\\ 1\\,080 & =15(2{a}_{1}+29d) \\\\ \\frac{1\\,080}{15} & =2{a}_{1}+29d \\\\ 72 & =2{a}_{1}+29d.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we can write an equation for the $17$th term of the arithmetic series  and substitute in its known value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\ {a}_{17} & ={a}_{1}+(17-1)d \\\\ 33 & ={a}_{1}+16d\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}33={a}_{1}+16d \\\\ 72=2{a}_{1}+29d\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we multiply the first equation by $-2$ and add the result to the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-66 & =-2{a}_{1}-32d \\\\ 72 & =2{a}_{1}+29d \\\\ 6 & =-3d \\\\ d & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $d=-2$ into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}33 & ={a}_{1}+16(-2) \\\\ 33 & ={a}_{1}-32 \\\\ {a}_{1} & =65.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396134 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087726 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d21d6066-f7c8-5a47-b150-d0ae25d2d60b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Center and Axis Lengths Given an Inhomogeneous Polynomial Equation of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f7f334c8-c362-5bc3-bb42-acf3a7888901" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396136} {:answer/id #uuid "306281a3-80c8-5e0a-9a37-cd54c88c73c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396137} {:answer/id #uuid "5b8c75df-6d72-5de6-8aa7-4da99784ebe0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396138} {:answer/id #uuid "b3f7d8da-7727-5a58-ab68-96bc81e72057" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396139} {:answer/id #uuid "ec7d741b-53e6-5079-a8a5-dd4f4717842f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396140}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "306281a3-80c8-5e0a-9a37-cd54c88c73c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396137} :answer-field/id #uuid "e14f3866-43ea-5b8f-9948-a2dc29883068" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396135}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fd2cdae2-5e26-5778-ab7e-65d79d794e32" :question/math-academy-id "q-7639" :question/problem "Consider the ellipse whose equation is ${x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-2x+12y=-11.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. Its center is $(1,-3)$\nII. The length of the major axis  is $2\\sqrt{2}$\nIII. The length of the minor axis is $4$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must group the $x$ and $y$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-2x+12y & =-11 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2x)+(2{y}^{2}+12y) & =-11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for both the $x$ and $y$ terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}-2x)+(2{y}^{2}+12y) & =-11 \\\\ [{x}^{2}-2x]+2[{y}^{2}+6y] & =-11 \\\\ [(x-1)^{2}-{1}^{2}]+2[(y+3)^{2}-{3}^{2}] & =-11 \\\\ [(x-1)^{2}-1]+2[(y+3)^{2}-9] & =-11 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}-1+2(y+3)^{2}-18 & =-11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rearrange the equation into the desired form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2}-1+2(y+3)^{2}-18 & =-11 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+2(y+3)^{2} & =-11+1+18 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+2(y+3)^{2} & =8 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{8}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{(2\\sqrt{2})^{2}}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2\\sqrt{2}>2,$ the length of the major axis is $2\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}=4\\sqrt{2},$ the length of the minor axis is $2\\cdot 2=4,$ and the ellipse is centered at $(1,-3).$\n\nTherefore,  I and III are true, and II is false."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403843 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087728 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a57ec9ff-99fd-5a3d-9a89-e76ded9d01cb" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the y-Intercepts of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a4e2843-1069-5d5a-bc66-06cba791d839" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-2\\pm \\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403845} {:answer/id #uuid "ee8990c4-efb5-5c40-8ee3-12b22397b390" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,1\\pm \\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403846} {:answer/id #uuid "05b80062-5158-567e-9a1e-8cfd58f9c9b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,2\\pm \\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403847} {:answer/id #uuid "d7aba08c-b98e-5626-b761-ad0e976d4fab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-2\\pm 2\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403848} {:answer/id #uuid "4d59c083-d711-58a1-a0a9-72a889180cdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,2\\pm \\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403849}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a4e2843-1069-5d5a-bc66-06cba791d839" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-2\\pm \\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403845} :answer-field/id #uuid "8df721af-644e-5936-b5f6-55c036b03023" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403844}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c9bca935-b9bf-540f-a535-f5de3e3b987f" :question/math-academy-id "q-7642" :question/problem "Calculate the points where the ellipse \n$\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{6}=1$ intercepts the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $y$-intercept, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $y.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{6} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(0-1)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{6} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{6} & =1 \\\\ 4+6(y+2)^{2} & =36 \\\\ 6(y+2)^{2} & =32 \\\\ 3(y+2)^{2} & =16 \\\\ (y+2)^{2} & =\\frac{16}{3} \\\\ y+2 & =\\pm \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ y & =-2\\pm \\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $y$-axis at the points $(0,-2-\\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{3})$ and $(0,-2+\\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{3}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396148 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087728 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a57ec9ff-99fd-5a3d-9a89-e76ded9d01cb" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the y-Intercepts of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bf73c45-3794-517e-8928-e6a008fbf5ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,3)$ and $(0,6)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396150} {:answer/id #uuid "65c8299f-0cca-5cc7-9f93-89e41aaadb17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-6)$ and $(0,3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396151} {:answer/id #uuid "a0731f5e-f8ac-5439-971f-5fe3dacb20b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0)$ and $(0,6)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396152} {:answer/id #uuid "75a247b7-4469-5470-aaaf-1d9daf6fb7f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-6)$ and $(0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396153} {:answer/id #uuid "6bac02c1-368e-5372-9c25-9ed8a24fc65d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\pm 6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396154}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75a247b7-4469-5470-aaaf-1d9daf6fb7f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-6)$ and $(0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396153} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2e54ba7-369d-573d-a4bf-97c68ce7831a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396149}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "86de3c58-7ec8-5280-8df9-9b9e42af2697" :question/math-academy-id "q-7644" :question/problem "Calculate the points where the ellipse \n$\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{12}=1$ intercepts the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $y$-intercept, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $y.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{12} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(0-2)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{12} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{4}{16}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{12} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{12} & =1 \\\\ 3+(y+3)^{2} & =12 \\\\ (y+3)^{2} & =9 \\\\ y+3 & =\\pm 3 \\\\ y & =-3\\pm 3 \\\\ y & =-6\\,,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $y$-axis at the points $(0,-6)$ and $(0,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403850 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087724 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6381faa4-5dfd-58fa-ace7-411fd2a97e98" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the x-Intercepts of an Ellipse By Completing the Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19a9a2f6-bd42-5579-a2ff-e314a223a8b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403852} {:answer/id #uuid "85bc7d3a-2417-530a-983c-0a9380f5af4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403853} {:answer/id #uuid "57970bb2-2ea8-522d-a5b9-0263776ebf54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2\\pm 3\\sqrt{2},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403854} {:answer/id #uuid "7aebdb04-492d-5e2c-828e-8c2f57c1636a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403855} {:answer/id #uuid "4072ec56-b787-50c7-a9df-ca77f8413053" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\pm \\sqrt{5},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403856}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7aebdb04-492d-5e2c-828e-8c2f57c1636a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403855} :answer-field/id #uuid "e32446d2-a32e-554b-89ca-512e15fbc942" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403851}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "82249adf-2c55-575e-90cb-7bba0c1d440e" :question/math-academy-id "q-7645" :question/problem "Calculate the points where the ellipse  $9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}-36x+16y=-16$ intercepts the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $x$-intercepts, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}-36x+16y & =-16 \\\\ 9{x}^{2}+4(0)^{2}-36x+16(0) & =-16 \\\\ 9{x}^{2}-36x & =-16 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x & =-\\frac{16}{9} \\\\ (x-2)^{2}-{2}^{2} & =-\\frac{16}{9} \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =-\\frac{16}{9}+4 \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =-\\frac{16}{9}+\\frac{36}{9} \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =\\frac{20}{9} \\\\ x-2 & =\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3} \\\\ x & =2\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at $(2-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3},0)$ and $(2+\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3},0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345221 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152117 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "92a06a13-9e6b-53b1-9fad-ec6cba8b9545" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LCM of Two Single-Variable Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d89e619-199e-55ac-8eca-9d77fffa7706" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30(y+1)^{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343023} {:answer/id #uuid "a00fafbc-22ed-52d6-8f01-31dffa057768" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30(y+1)^{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343651} {:answer/id #uuid "da747809-5242-5b07-9971-7285cceb6b5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "150(y+1)^{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344157} {:answer/id #uuid "eb3e407c-9b56-57b2-833f-24e819c942f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30(y+1)^{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344298} {:answer/id #uuid "eb9d1027-57d3-5a23-9b93-f847e9338320" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "150(y+1)^{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344302}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d89e619-199e-55ac-8eca-9d77fffa7706" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30(y+1)^{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343023} :answer-field/id #uuid "81b1b5e2-12f3-59b8-8a06-c39c41de6a36" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344722}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6fe6c820-9683-5a7a-b33c-5f323f0359af" :question/math-academy-id "q-76457" :question/problem "Find the least common multiple of $10(y+1)^{6}$ and $15(y+1)^{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the least common multiple ($\\text{LCM}$) of two polynomials that are made up of products of binomials, we find the least common multiple of the constants and the least common multiple of the variable part. Then, we multiply all of the results together.\n\nSo first, we find $\\text{LCM}(10,15):$\n\n- Multiples of $10:$ $\\,10,\\,20,\\,30,\\,40,…$\n\n- Multiples of $15:$ $\\,15,\\,30,\\,45,…$\n\nSo, $\\text{LCM}(10,15)=30.$\n\nTo find the $\\text{LCM}$ of $(y+1)^{6}$ and $(y+1)^{4},$ we raise $(y+1)$ to the largest power that appears in the expressions, which is $6.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}((y+1)^{6},\\,(y+1)^{4})=(y+1)^{6}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply all of our results together:\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(10(y+1)^{6},\\,15(y+1)^{4})=30\\cdot (y+1)^{6}=30(y+1)^{6}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403857 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087729 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fea426db-a854-5efc-a0cd-7d52019a7cc6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the y-Intercepts of an Ellipse By Completing the Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d8ffd5a2-cc34-54ff-9dff-23dc300f6355" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-1\\pm \\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403859} {:answer/id #uuid "d0c7d047-707f-5931-9d5e-9a61b60ed7e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,1\\pm \\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403860} {:answer/id #uuid "f83190fc-dc05-547f-bf1b-da6929340b16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-1\\pm \\sqrt{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403861} {:answer/id #uuid "9180fb06-deed-5622-9444-9a79d51d72e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-1\\pm 2\\sqrt{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403862} {:answer/id #uuid "3643a336-82e3-5461-b332-ac90d59fc7d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,1\\pm \\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403863}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f83190fc-dc05-547f-bf1b-da6929340b16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-1\\pm \\sqrt{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403861} :answer-field/id #uuid "651c5733-a40e-59c7-b992-154c6de03a88" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403858}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d4ad8b92-e3b5-5a56-bfac-e69e0f571229" :question/math-academy-id "q-7646" :question/problem "Determine the points where the ellipse ${x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-4x+4y=10$ intercepts the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $y$-intercepts, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and try to solve the resulting equation for $y.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-4x+4y & =10 \\\\ (0)^{2}+2{y}^{2}-4(0)+4y & =10 \\\\ 2{y}^{2}+4y & =10 \\\\ {y}^{2}+2y & =5 \\\\ (y+1)^{2}-{1}^{2} & =5 \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =5+1 \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =6 \\\\ y+1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{6} \\\\ y & =-1\\pm \\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,-1-\\sqrt{6})$ and $(0,-1+\\sqrt{6}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345222 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152119 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "57496436-8bd0-567e-8c37-ed584849ce1a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LCM of Three Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0605e45f-3e5a-5d33-a682-7100aa2a8ca5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120{x}^{4}(y+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342294} {:answer/id #uuid "11f59668-bc57-5e29-9418-45b2197c07c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120{x}^{7}(y+2)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342398} {:answer/id #uuid "b54ac959-62ea-5199-9f5f-2f2c86259b21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60{x}^{17}(y+2)^{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343825} {:answer/id #uuid "ca0e194f-07bb-5959-8ccc-8a01a267e3db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60{x}^{4}(y+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344013} {:answer/id #uuid "d99fcded-2ea6-5c5d-aeda-dffd3b0f7c7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60{x}^{7}(y+2)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344149}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d99fcded-2ea6-5c5d-aeda-dffd3b0f7c7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60{x}^{7}(y+2)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344149} :answer-field/id #uuid "27415d0b-998e-58eb-abc2-faf32c0e2a98" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344563}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "288f9264-3705-5ed8-96fa-98d1f57e896b" :question/math-academy-id "q-76475" :question/problem "Find the least common multiple of the polynomials $20{x}^{7}(y+2),$ $\\,10{x}^{6}(y+2)^{3},$ and $12{x}^{4}(y+2)^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the least common multiple ($\\text{LCM}$) of three polynomials that are made up of products of binomials, we find the least common multiple of the constants and the least common multiple of each variable part. Then, we multiply all of the results together.\n\nSo first, we find $\\text{LCM}(20,10,12):$\n\n- Multiples of $20:$ $\\,20,\\,40,\\,60,\\,80,\\,100,\\,…$\n\n- Multiples of $10:$ $\\,10,\\,20,\\,30,\\,40,\\,50,\\,60,\\,70,\\,…$\n\n- Multiples of $12:$ $\\,12,\\,24,\\,36,\\,48,\\,60,\\,72,…$\n\nSo, $\\text{LCM}(20,10,12)=60.$\n\nTo find the $\\text{LCM}$ of ${x}^{7}(y+2),{x}^{6}(y+2)^{3},$ and ${x}^{4}(y+2)^{2},$ we find the largest powers for $x$ and $(y+2)$ that appear in all these expressions:\n\n- For ${x}^{7},\\,{x}^{6},$ and ${x}^{4},$ the largest power is $7.$\n\n- For $(y+2),(y+2)^{3},$ and $(y+2)^{2},$ the largest power is $3.$\n\nNow, we raise $x$ and $(y+2)$ to the corresponding largest powers. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}({x}^{7}(y+2),\\,{x}^{6}(y+2)^{3},\\,{x}^{4}(y+2)^{2})={x}^{7}\\cdot (y+2)^{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply all of our results together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{LCM}(20{x}^{7}(y+2),\\,10{x}^{6}(y+2)^{3},\\,12{x}^{4}(y+2)^{2}) & = \\\\ 60\\cdot {x}^{7}\\cdot (y+2)^{3} & = \\\\ 60{x}^{7}(y+2)^{3} & .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345227 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115220 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "387e57ad-6f80-5b90-8adf-7bf05618b532" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Secant with Non-Special Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b458ab9-f8d2-5847-a1d7-a82898766471" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342334} {:answer/id #uuid "20197b9b-a175-536e-b070-6b0342f0432b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342502} {:answer/id #uuid "4e63fcf2-15c2-57a2-9183-f2e3d764402c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342883} {:answer/id #uuid "5c5024a8-ad7b-5afe-b439-92410b5aeb10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343012} {:answer/id #uuid "7639ae17-8091-53b1-8ba7-0966fa92e843" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343259}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b458ab9-f8d2-5847-a1d7-a82898766471" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342334} :answer-field/id #uuid "78baf57b-c872-5926-9e6f-da7c5cccc697" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344712}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "20b45040-332b-5b0d-9817-efef67df2755" :question/math-academy-id "q-76490" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=6$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to one decimal place?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value of $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{6})=1.403\\,348…≈1.403\\,3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $1.403\\,3$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=1.403\\,3.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=1.403\\,3.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=1.403\\,3.$\n\n![](images/9b/9b3d60be42ec916f0e62f3a86cb1ce2aa628482c97f68f0df7c6ab533b919102.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -1.403\\,3 \\\\  & ≈4.879\\,9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}≈1.403\\,3\\,\\cdot 4.879\\,9≈6.8\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399057 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115220 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "387e57ad-6f80-5b90-8adf-7bf05618b532" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Secant with Non-Special Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f6b8877-60d4-55b2-8bac-f93e30946ac6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27\\,013" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399059} {:answer/id #uuid "cd75ca22-d221-528e-9374-a2127cff3475" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "31\\,805" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399060} {:answer/id #uuid "c98b22aa-0045-5d2d-826a-877753c65569" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,156" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399061} {:answer/id #uuid "0a6a6bc1-55ab-55da-a856-6d2b5f1bdd88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21\\,642" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399062} {:answer/id #uuid "d2fc604b-14af-553c-9b93-ef74d9892f1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\,681" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399063}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f6b8877-60d4-55b2-8bac-f93e30946ac6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27\\,013" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399059} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc8f8da8-d591-560f-9fd3-2166ffc07f23" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399058}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "21067b43-552c-57fe-bdc4-8f094139d29a" :question/math-academy-id "q-76493" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-\\frac{7}{2}$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to the nearest integer?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x},$ therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-\\frac{2}{7}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{2}{7})=106.601\\,549{…}^{∘}≈{106.602}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${106.602}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={106.602}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={73.398}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={73.398}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/e3/e378560486eca17f870b21743718c9f984f2bd3bf5609601f1cea674e15018e6.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{73.398}^{∘} \\\\  & ={253.398}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=106.602\\cdot 253.398≈27\\,013,\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403864 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115181 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9a3d8db-a6e0-5c96-9c75-415d4a75cf4d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations With No Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab9ab64a-0cd3-5dc1-8bf9-3e5e921cb9ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403866} {:answer/id #uuid "bba7a0a8-4e9b-57ba-ac88-a9232adfbc0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403867} {:answer/id #uuid "d0100248-920c-515e-a5f9-c80112f0657a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403868} {:answer/id #uuid "583cbb8c-6819-5a17-9eea-b75d06519784" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403869} {:answer/id #uuid "2a3f912e-91b5-5c0f-b650-babdfaadb19e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403870}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bba7a0a8-4e9b-57ba-ac88-a9232adfbc0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403867} :answer-field/id #uuid "50a2c6d2-6a4a-5a62-a1fb-a094e9a83f71" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403865}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a83238f9-a8b1-5193-9f12-9adee2517d46" :question/math-academy-id "q-76503" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has no solutions for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}?$\n\nI. $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=0$ $$\nII. $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=8$\nIII. $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{7}{3}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the range of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=k$ has no solutions for $k\\in (-1,1).$ \n\nTherefore, of the given equations, only equation I has no solutions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345249 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100820 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aa20dbfa-3f6f-5caf-9c10-906f2422f161" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a Trigonometric Equation Containing Tangent or Cotangent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "549eb3ff-0d70-5d98-95ae-140c946d9434" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{5}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342942} {:answer/id #uuid "71f52d6b-9b86-50ff-8f7e-cfe37e69e6cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{3\\pi }{8}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343224} {:answer/id #uuid "9788f504-fc7f-5f00-9fe6-ff56fdf4863f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{8}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343567} {:answer/id #uuid "b6219bdb-a8f7-50e4-9cf6-285351b7c0ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343834} {:answer/id #uuid "f41eacee-7b5a-5190-8daf-3e7d61906f13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344386}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71f52d6b-9b86-50ff-8f7e-cfe37e69e6cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{3\\pi }{8}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343224} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c72e057-5521-52b8-8355-dcc75ede7d96" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344647}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e4b36406-0f67-50c9-b15a-12ee8329088e" :question/math-academy-id "q-76741" :question/problem "What is the general solution to the equation $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}+1}?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer.\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{8})=1+\\sqrt{2}.$*" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}+1} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =\\sqrt{2}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\text{arctan}(\\sqrt{2}+1)=\\frac{3\\pi }{8}\n$$\n\nThe tangent function has a period of $\\pi .$  Hence, the general solution for the given equation is\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3\\pi }{8}+n\\pi .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345255 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115358 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "51739685-2d53-5c9b-870b-b557ffdf2768" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Area of a Triangle by Solving for a Variable Given a Trigonometric Ratio of One Angle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74a1f4a2-14fe-58a7-8d95-3369048da2c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343246} {:answer/id #uuid "8d168dd0-b3c3-576d-90ff-6db8eda26288" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343471} {:answer/id #uuid "96e525fb-0817-5487-aa3b-b0e69dfcf066" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343558} {:answer/id #uuid "a521d3f7-e12f-5f7c-8950-f5892e2df150" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "105" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343695} {:answer/id #uuid "c9795681-7425-5697-a020-bca804b477be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "80" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344006}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9795681-7425-5697-a020-bca804b477be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "80" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344006} :answer-field/id #uuid "be3dea8a-e41f-53a0-a63d-3c221e2798e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344828}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "79db05b6-9295-5a92-a7f0-878c0e6e3e21" :question/math-academy-id "q-76770" :question/problem "![](images/f1/f1880dc62f774152ce5076a313928c0e8109ca174184704c5880891ed9243718.png)\n\nGiven that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(m∠A)=\\frac{2}{5},$ calculate the area (in square units) of $△ABC$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "In this case, the area of the right triangle is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}AC\\cdot BC.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(m∠A)=\\frac{2}{5}.$ Notice that relative to $∠A,$ we have expressions for the lengths of the opposite side $\\overset{―}{BC}$ and adjacent side $\\overset{―}{AC}.$ Hence, we can use the tangent ratio to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(m∠A) & =\\frac{BC}{AC} \\\\ \\frac{2}{5} & =\\frac{2x+4}{6x+8} \\\\ 2(6x+8) & =5(2x+4) \\\\ 12x+16 & =10x+20 \\\\ 12x-10x & =20-16 \\\\ 2x & =4 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the side lengths are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =2x+4=2(2)+4=8, \\\\ AC & =6x+8=6(2)+8=20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of $△ABC$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}AC\\cdot BC \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 20\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =80.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345256 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115360 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe83dedc-3034-5e3a-b546-dd57c0ccc6e8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Area of a Triangle by Solving for a Variable Given an Angle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11080520-591d-552f-a2b5-376652328032" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342388} {:answer/id #uuid "20d23199-1707-5c8a-9c95-20a93346eafc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342508} {:answer/id #uuid "23574e97-dff3-5da7-b5f5-3cada9731425" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342530} {:answer/id #uuid "bb1702de-fd43-50d8-a686-676b7d54dfb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343884} {:answer/id #uuid "f9127ae7-e1de-5695-9e51-049f89115cde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344429}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9127ae7-e1de-5695-9e51-049f89115cde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344429} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b740c15-4310-51b1-b74c-cbe3e504200f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344566}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c61332e0-ca58-508b-96a8-0bfc3b0688b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-76773" :question/problem "![](images/ca/ca9c6640b6cb239ae19b195731d8f605adae8826f66e10b075f7b0ad4a60690f.png)\n\nFind the area (in square units) of $△ABC$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "In this case, the area of the right triangle is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}AB\\cdot BC.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $m∠A={30}^{∘}.$ Notice that relative to $∠A,$ we have expressions for the lengths of the opposite side $\\overset{―}{BC}$ and adjacent side $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ Hence, we can use the tangent ratio to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(m∠A) & =\\frac{BC}{AB} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡({30}^{∘}) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\,x}{x+4} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\,x}{x+4} \\\\ \\sqrt{3}(x+4) & =3\\cdot \\sqrt{3}\\,x \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\,x+4\\sqrt{3} & =3\\sqrt{3}\\,x \\\\ -2\\sqrt{3}\\,x & =-4\\sqrt{3} \\\\ x & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{2\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the side lengths are \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =\\sqrt{3}\\,x=\\sqrt{3}(2)=2\\sqrt{3}, \\\\ AB & =x+4=2+4=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of $△ABC$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot AB\\cdot BC \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6\\cdot 2\\sqrt{3} \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345259 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119860 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1889ba1a-09d4-5021-9ed2-3e531f8d5ef0" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Arc Length of a Three-Dimensional Spatial Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "107d5a2a-4f6f-52c4-a7ea-a3c986f47360" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342381} {:answer/id #uuid "12208fda-53a7-5d69-9f6a-dbdffb153e06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342399} {:answer/id #uuid "768320a8-bc68-56f3-9b17-f4dad32ac2f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343266} {:answer/id #uuid "9381484a-6981-59d3-b123-6f51531c898a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343530} {:answer/id #uuid "bd7ef7a7-f907-538f-b138-4c1ddb333884" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343904}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "107d5a2a-4f6f-52c4-a7ea-a3c986f47360" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342381} :answer-field/id #uuid "20642a04-5ca1-5221-ae4c-b1d1be5781d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344550}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "06fe26ef-4ec2-5c23-a26d-637e986a7e20" :question/math-academy-id "q-76906" :question/problem "Find the arc length of the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2},\\,4,\\,{t}^{2}⟩$\nfrom  $t=0$ to $t=2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t,\\,0,\\,2t⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(2t)}^{2}+0+{(2t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\,t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $t\\ge 0$ for $t\\in [0,2].$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2\\sqrt{2}t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}({t}^{2}){|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373230 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119860 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1889ba1a-09d4-5021-9ed2-3e531f8d5ef0" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Arc Length of a Three-Dimensional Spatial Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ffcfd43-b15b-55e0-9453-963134a6d995" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "130" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373232} {:answer/id #uuid "2e9391e4-7424-54d0-a36c-f5b21aa3b4e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "150" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373233} {:answer/id #uuid "726ddffe-91b8-5f33-bfb8-b9930883abab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "125" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373234} {:answer/id #uuid "4048f826-7e00-5c3f-ad4e-3b51a78539bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373235} {:answer/id #uuid "4ab3aac1-31a4-5103-a8fc-ee5e47c2090b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373236}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ffcfd43-b15b-55e0-9453-963134a6d995" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "130" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373232} :answer-field/id #uuid "f96bcd49-a02f-5bd7-ab21-7a05bacf2ad7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373231}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5e0a56e4-79a9-58b5-babc-ce3937cee4f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-76910" :question/problem "Find the arc length of the curve $r(t)=⟨\\frac{4-\\sqrt{3}{t}^{2}}{\\sqrt{2}},\\,{t}^{3},\\,t+5⟩$\nfrom  $t=0$ to $t=5.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4-\\sqrt{3}{t}^{2}}{\\sqrt{2}}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+5)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\sqrt{6}t,\\,3{t}^{2},\\,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(-\\sqrt{6}t)}^{2}+{(3{t}^{2})}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6{t}^{2}+9{t}^{4}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(1+3{t}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =1+3{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}(1+3{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(t+{t}^{3}){|}_{0}^{5} \\\\  & =5+125-0 \\\\  & =130.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345262 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087670 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "12ff17ae-9319-5498-a5e0-1d49ddbef50a" :knowledge-point/title "Using Alternative Forms of Double-Angle Formula to Simplify Expressions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a0075a5-c212-5332-8254-ebacab091f7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡{44}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342847} {:answer/id #uuid "579bc87b-bc15-55d2-9b88-65df7df9f603" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡{22}^{∘}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{22}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342969} {:answer/id #uuid "83360fb2-68cd-5170-8292-0cf16f4cf726" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{cos}^{2}⁡{44}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343361} {:answer/id #uuid "dd6b13d2-42b5-583d-8200-ed5b37808ec0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡{44}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344182} {:answer/id #uuid "fb1a1f8b-f9f2-5fba-9b53-4159efa314f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡{25}^{∘}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{25}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344446}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a0075a5-c212-5332-8254-ebacab091f7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡{44}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342847} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6797f23-b08c-5f40-9334-1a28a80d78c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344925}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ec9090ba-bb1f-5578-9f23-889023ae580e" :question/math-academy-id "q-7692" :question/problem "Express $2{cos}^{2}⁡{22}^{∘}-1$ as a single trigonometric ratio." :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n2{cos}^{2}⁡x-1=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={22}^{∘}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{cos}^{2}⁡{22}^{∘}-1 & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\cdot {22}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡{44}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345288 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119744 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1e310cbe-9d6b-5bc4-960a-8813333bc8be" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Arc Length of a Planar Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ffe2816-aae7-59ca-9562-412dd652a6c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342376} {:answer/id #uuid "2bb81157-2ab8-5ec8-a9fa-335547e6606a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342605} {:answer/id #uuid "519f8d58-aa96-5048-9d82-8885955e1cd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342913} {:answer/id #uuid "5ee0276d-41f4-5591-b923-85e85120cdc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343039} {:answer/id #uuid "988c1583-846c-502f-b92b-7cb927f59f72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343579}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ffe2816-aae7-59ca-9562-412dd652a6c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342376} :answer-field/id #uuid "890c712d-24f5-52cb-9fc0-29be52e23fa4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344732}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "707c9a96-8c3c-57b1-ae5a-3037250afe04" :question/math-academy-id "q-77220" :question/problem "![](images/2e/2efa774a3a7e97c23f695cfbdf681b53df055b8a84b84e2729af0a565841aeb7.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t-2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩$\nfrom  $t=1$ to $t=2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t-2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,,4t\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(4t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)}^{2}+{(4t\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{t}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡t+16{t}^{2}{sin}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =4t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $t>0$ for $t\\in [1,2].$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}4t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{t}^{2}{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =2(4-1) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393688 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105897 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6dee200-f517-5645-87ec-ccf36012e55e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Vector Equation of the Line Passing Through Two Given Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3dabd2cd-e323-5103-b1ab-dd6de852acfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393690} {:answer/id #uuid "3fbeffbc-a98d-56e3-a819-4d71d19c1260" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393691} {:answer/id #uuid "270c04c6-7ed5-5a17-9a85-ba8df2eaa132" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393692} {:answer/id #uuid "1bb321c2-64e7-5bb6-9fd0-371d482d1d6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393693} {:answer/id #uuid "58796e97-300e-5e5a-9b65-29e119ff59f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393694}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3fbeffbc-a98d-56e3-a819-4d71d19c1260" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393691} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f0788af-fd78-553e-8567-a457572ce6fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393689}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e77266e3-e110-5646-9023-0f09c4f4c827" :question/math-academy-id "q-77433" :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the line that passes through the points $A(1,1,0)$ and $B(3,-1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "The vector equation of the straight line passing through $A$ and parallel to $v$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=a+tv,\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $a$ is the position vector of $A.$ \n\nWe can find a direction vector by computing the displacement vector between the given points $A(1,1,0)$ and $B(3,-1,2)$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{AB} \\\\  & =⟨3,-1,2⟩-⟨1,1,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,-2,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector equation of the line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403871 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105898 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c338d1eb-7e13-5089-8dd3-d5ca6eefd4ba" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points on a Line That Are a Fixed Distance From Another Point on the Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2d05b78-acf4-5f5e-a4a5-a47b385be2af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${Q}_{1}(6,2,2)$ and ${Q}_{2}(1,0,-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403873} {:answer/id #uuid "e86a9368-3e01-579c-a45b-27cec0740826" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${Q}_{1}(4,2,6)$ and ${Q}_{2}(1,0,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403874} {:answer/id #uuid "243227bd-445a-5c74-9b23-fe624173fb6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${Q}_{1}(2,0,6)$ and ${Q}_{2}(0,2,-4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403875} {:answer/id #uuid "0312db3e-2587-592d-bac1-fd0950f055c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${Q}_{1}(2,0,6)$ and ${Q}_{2}(-1,3,-9)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403876} {:answer/id #uuid "d156c13a-c459-55bc-8fae-a2511dac06a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${Q}_{1}(3,-1,11)$ and ${Q}_{2}(0,2,-4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403877}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "243227bd-445a-5c74-9b23-fe624173fb6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${Q}_{1}(2,0,6)$ and ${Q}_{2}(0,2,-4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403875} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e8713f5-ca93-55c6-99f3-5140f5065e48" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403872}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ca1265ac-23c6-5e5b-be9c-b56a47160343" :question/math-academy-id "q-77443" :question/problem "Consider the straight line that passes through $P(1,1,1)$ and is parallel to $v=⟨1,-1,5⟩.$ Find all the possible points $Q$ on that line such that $PQ=3\\sqrt{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vector equation of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =⟨1,1,1⟩+t⟨1,-1,5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSince $Q$ lies on the line, we know that there must be a real number $t$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OQ} & =⟨1,1,1⟩+t⟨1,-1,5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =(⟨1,1,1⟩+t⟨1,-1,5⟩)-⟨1,1,1⟩ \\\\  & =t\\,⟨1,-1,5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t,\\,-t,\\,5t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $PQ=3\\sqrt{3},$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}PQ & =3\\sqrt{3} \\\\ |\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| & =3\\sqrt{3} \\\\ |⟨t,\\,-t,\\,5t⟩| & =3\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\sqrt{{t}^{2}+(-t)^{2}+(5t)^{2}} & =3\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\sqrt{27{t}^{2}} & =3\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\pm 3\\sqrt{3}t & =3\\sqrt{3} \\\\ t & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the values $t=\\pm 1$ into the equation for the line, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{O{Q}_{1}}=⟨1,1,1⟩+⟨1,-1,5⟩=⟨2,0,6⟩, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{O{Q}_{2}}=⟨1,1,1⟩-⟨1,-1,5⟩=⟨0,2,-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the points are ${Q}_{1}(2,0,6)$ and ${Q}_{2}(0,2,-4).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235582 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235285 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "58a49d00-cc58-5228-af38-9369cf31d8c8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Scalar Factor When Direction Reverses"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ab5b44a-6f14-5d8c-b994-0ff723092bf6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234586} {:answer/id #uuid "2b5b4c72-e340-52b5-be53-6903f63361a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234611} {:answer/id #uuid "cbe36434-9519-583f-9b69-c587e4937715" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234866} {:answer/id #uuid "ce1ea7c1-61ca-56ba-a7d9-91573e3d6a5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234869} {:answer/id #uuid "f46ca5b6-1fd3-597e-9657-e4197a5f9d69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234933}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ab5b44a-6f14-5d8c-b994-0ff723092bf6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234586} :answer-field/id #uuid "1aa48e98-a238-5102-8717-85e261cb4e21" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234997}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "adcdd06d-1586-51df-9df4-c005428c239d" :question/math-academy-id "q-77451" :question/problem "![](images/28/28faea3a553c505a8a5ab9bc7dd6e784dd23a47c336c70d31218fbe249d57cff.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=\\lambda a+3b,$ find $\\lambda .$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a+3b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $3b.$ So, we first draw the vector $3b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/52/52421034c2919620a0a9aae844db0eba2d396e5d6f1152d69bee40ff56c4d8ad.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $3b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=3|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=-3a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =3b-3a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+3b=-3a+3b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-3\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403878 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096652 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "45b6a076-8795-5510-aabc-62a925826dd0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Scalar Factors in a Linear Combination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a9d3aac-2ddf-558e-9987-0dee3c2852ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-2.5$ and $\\mu =-3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403880} {:answer/id #uuid "d0c5e6ea-81c8-564a-84fa-99f998a1a61d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =2.5$ and $\\mu =3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403881} {:answer/id #uuid "ed44e8da-0848-535d-a8ae-87bdbaa1daf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =2.5$ and $\\mu =-3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403882} {:answer/id #uuid "8353c3d6-6092-5f38-bfd8-8a8168b1859c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-3$ and $\\mu =2.5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403883} {:answer/id #uuid "aa67f058-afcd-51ee-97d4-c377ce896d7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-2.5$ and $\\mu =3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403884}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0c5e6ea-81c8-564a-84fa-99f998a1a61d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =2.5$ and $\\mu =3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403881} :answer-field/id #uuid "6554496f-6084-5a58-8758-7a159d646048" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403879}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b8cb8830-d33b-5725-abf1-a6d3f309ef27" :question/math-academy-id "q-77452" :question/problem "![](images/16/1649e6c4ef2874cda2a7d7428b54220ef2a1790ba4b197c755165c48add0c31e.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=\\lambda a+\\mu b$, find $\\lambda $ and $\\mu .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/7c/7cf17440c27765efb34b6a01c12a76ad4e980fc540307e2218227153661db5dd.png)\n\nIn $△OAC$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OA=2.5|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OA}$ and $a$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OA}=2.5a.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $AC=3|b|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $b$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}=3b.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\  & =2.5a+3b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+\\mu b=2.5a+3b.$ \n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n$\\,$ $\\lambda =2.5$ and $\\mu =3$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345298 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059800 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a827b0d0-faa2-521f-b166-e13873b1da00" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Entry of the Product of Two Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e87020f-a1a2-5550-b424-ca8a36fb5e47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342885} {:answer/id #uuid "4ec62960-9b9f-5dbe-91ab-850722f4e729" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342887} {:answer/id #uuid "64180b08-3f71-59dd-90e0-90dea28ee95e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343089} {:answer/id #uuid "91130e08-082e-5cb7-a2c7-4dd80867866a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343507} {:answer/id #uuid "bc7761b5-0a6c-5936-8c5a-de0e4a27cf87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343895}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ec62960-9b9f-5dbe-91ab-850722f4e729" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342887} :answer-field/id #uuid "a00a19b4-db54-5703-b836-7c731faeed36" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344776}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6eeac7ee-9286-57c4-a413-7511fe459e52" :question/math-academy-id "q-77461" :question/problem "If $P=QR$, where \n$Q=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 4 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 6 & -3\\end{aligned}]$\nand \n$R=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 6 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 2 & -4\\end{aligned}]$, then ${p}_{34}=$" :question/worked-solution "Multiplying the $3$rd row in $Q$ by the $4$th column in $R$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}_{34} & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 & 4 & -5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-1)+(-2)\\cdot 4+4\\cdot 2+(-5)\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =-4-8+8+20 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345301 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100871 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a0a4fa3d-fc1a-5e95-a632-f3a25c9bc4b1" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Dimension of the Product of Two Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a7f860f4-816a-5393-ae9d-2ecb0c1f468c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The product is undefined" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343716} {:answer/id #uuid "af921b50-e88f-5ebd-b3e2-dba22eb65847" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\times 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343782} {:answer/id #uuid "bd8558fc-a4cc-539d-b781-9288c5ab8bdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\times 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343906} {:answer/id #uuid "c2bd3920-bd82-5ac0-9594-335890c16575" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\times 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343952} {:answer/id #uuid "ec6ccab9-827b-5ef9-8d9f-1b7428deeac4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\times 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344314}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec6ccab9-827b-5ef9-8d9f-1b7428deeac4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\times 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344314} :answer-field/id #uuid "dae74c21-b257-59e4-b24c-655b2133233b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344870}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9e32d224-c2ec-5642-95ee-f28fd98abe68" :question/math-academy-id "q-77475" :question/problem "Let\n$Y=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$U=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}].$\nWhat are the dimensions of $YU?$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that $Y$ is a $1\\times 3$ matrix and $U$ is a $3\\times 1$ matrix. \n\nThere are $3$ columns in $Y$ and $3$ rows in $U.$ Since the number of columns in $Y$ equals the number of rows in $U,$ the matrices $Y$ and $U$ are conformable for multiplication. \n\nThe product $YU$ has dimensions $1\\times 1:$\n\n$$\n{Y}_{1\\times \\boxed{\\text{3}}}{\\times }_{✓}{U}_{\\boxed{\\text{3}}\\times 1}={YU}_{1\\times 1}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403885 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100871 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a0a4fa3d-fc1a-5e95-a632-f3a25c9bc4b1" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Dimension of the Product of Two Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60592584-71a9-5dab-888c-405420e985f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The product is undefined" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403887} {:answer/id #uuid "803bf64e-e7ce-576c-8d72-84fb237b0900" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\times 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403888} {:answer/id #uuid "1d361957-5943-53dc-96fc-7cd9606514fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\times 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403889} {:answer/id #uuid "458d1885-5503-53b8-ada5-bebd8ce461d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\times 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403890} {:answer/id #uuid "a526ff5b-3dc1-5b6f-891e-1cefb24bf872" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\times 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403891}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "458d1885-5503-53b8-ada5-bebd8ce461d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\times 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403890} :answer-field/id #uuid "3bc81805-e6d6-540d-8f35-85fe2c75a075" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403886}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9bc6ac30-b2f0-52c3-a082-54b213e2ff6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-77476" :question/problem "Let\n$E=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$R=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\sqrt{3} & 1\\end{aligned}].$\nWhat are the dimensions of $ER?$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that $E$ is a $3\\times 1$ matrix and $R$ is a $1\\times 3$ matrix. \n\nThere is $1$ column in $E$ and $1$ row in $R.$ Since the number of columns in $E$ equals the number of rows in $R,$ the matrices $E$ and $R$ are conformable for multiplication. \n\nThe product $ER$ has dimensions $3\\times 3:$\n\n$$\n{E}_{3\\times \\boxed{\\text{1}}}{\\times }_{✓}{R}_{\\boxed{\\text{1}}\\times 3}={ER}_{3\\times 3}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345302 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059848 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "32df8909-a239-5167-b8ea-412f7cdaea93" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Singular Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13280805-29d2-5042-b55a-faeede02c8f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342406} {:answer/id #uuid "2cba85d6-597d-5cff-9587-d8777843feff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342613} {:answer/id #uuid "57ffe353-6941-56e7-afb2-8672ab9e51ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342972} {:answer/id #uuid "d63a3357-e696-58cd-82f4-a041327215d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344115} {:answer/id #uuid "fe3ce905-6030-53b3-b4a6-f3acab3ea132" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344475}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57ffe353-6941-56e7-afb2-8672ab9e51ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342972} :answer-field/id #uuid "43aa6600-ff87-53fc-af02-029b3f9c0fe0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344602}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a70a69db-90d3-5dde-88c1-3f25b19af419" :question/math-academy-id "q-77477" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $S$ given below?\n\n- $S$ is **not** a singular matrix\n- $S$ is a singular matrix\n- $det(S)=\\sqrt{3}$\n\n$$\nS=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & \\,0 \\\\ 2 & \\sqrt{3} & \\,\\,-1 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 4 & \\,\\,-\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(S) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & \\,0 \\\\ 2 & \\sqrt{3} & \\,\\,-1 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 4 & \\,\\,-\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1|\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & \\,\\,-1 \\\\ 4 & \\,\\,-\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}|-2|\\begin{aligned}2 & \\,-1 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & \\,-\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}|+0|\\begin{aligned}2 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(-{(\\sqrt{3})}^{2}+4)-2(-2\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{3})+0 \\\\  & =(-3+4)-2\\cdot (-\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =1+2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(S)=1+2\\sqrt{3}\\ne 0$, the matrix is **not ** singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement I is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345306 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119773 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1637e23-6566-5e45-af4c-b27333f882d1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Determinant Using a Laplace Expansion Across a Row"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "703f38f5-d562-5737-a3e9-455a97a42f39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343204} {:answer/id #uuid "917467d4-68ad-589a-b372-c2656ddfbe56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343511} {:answer/id #uuid "95733d44-1f56-5609-980e-fc36d7c34c02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343545} {:answer/id #uuid "b84505eb-59e1-5ba6-848a-5b83870be4a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343855} {:answer/id #uuid "fb9247e7-0c47-5cc3-ac67-d7bc0bbe7d48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344451}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "917467d4-68ad-589a-b372-c2656ddfbe56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343511} :answer-field/id #uuid "dfb3dcc7-ea2d-577b-bed7-1da5dd43872a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344884}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7219fa5b-320d-5221-9146-e95094c6f719" :question/math-academy-id "q-77487" :question/problem "Compute the following determinant using a cofactor expansion along the $1$st row:\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}|.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using cofactor expansion along the $1$st row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}| & =1\\cdot {C}_{11}+0\\cdot {C}_{12}+0\\cdot {C}_{13}+(-1)\\cdot {C}_{14} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{1+1}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}|+(-1)\\cdot (-1)^{1+4}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}|+|\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the first $3\\times 3$ determinant by applying a Laplace expansion along the $2$nd row, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}| & =1\\cdot {C}_{21}+5\\cdot {C}_{22}+0\\cdot {C}_{23} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{2+1}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}|+5\\cdot (-1)^{2+2}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -4 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}|+5|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -4 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(-3-0)+5(-6-4) \\\\  & =3-50 \\\\  & =-47\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the second  $3\\times 3$ determinant by applying a Laplace expansion along the $2$nd row, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}| & =0\\cdot {C}_{21}+1\\cdot {C}_{22}+5\\cdot {C}_{23} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{2+2}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}|+5\\cdot (-1)^{2+3}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}|-5|\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(0-2)-5(-8-4) \\\\  & =-2+60 \\\\  & =58\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}|=-47+58=11.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393716 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119774 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "db6f57f3-d9f0-5ea7-a557-e729a8c0777f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Determinant Using a Laplace Expansion Across a Column"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56aa1b0a-d3ba-5e9d-bf2f-3fee54a0aeb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & \\sqrt{3} & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}|-\\sqrt{5}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393718} {:answer/id #uuid "7fb2414b-474d-5931-bc91-ef0c7c308a57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393719} {:answer/id #uuid "0a75555d-2651-5755-856d-2004ab1df45f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 1\\end{aligned}|-\\sqrt{5}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393720} {:answer/id #uuid "0b28dfa1-9b13-5267-a03d-29a3b7303759" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 1\\end{aligned}|+\\sqrt{5}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393721} {:answer/id #uuid "4d361d0a-4eaf-51ec-b617-bbfea97f6c1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 1\\end{aligned}|-\\sqrt{5}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 5 & \\sqrt{5} & 1\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393722}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a75555d-2651-5755-856d-2004ab1df45f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 1\\end{aligned}|-\\sqrt{5}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393720} :answer-field/id #uuid "b752084c-ea22-57eb-b56c-359cc3f7787d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393717}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3ec0fb73-379b-5a1f-918f-29c718e49441" :question/math-academy-id "q-77501" :question/problem "$|\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & \\sqrt{3} & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 5 & \\sqrt{5} & 1\\end{aligned}|=$" :question/worked-solution "Using a cofactor expansion along the $3$rd column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & \\sqrt{3} & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 5 & \\sqrt{5} & 1\\end{aligned}| & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot {C}_{13}+0\\cdot {C}_{23}+0\\cdot {C}_{33}+\\sqrt{5}\\cdot {C}_{43} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot (-1)^{1+3}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 1\\end{aligned}|+\\sqrt{5}\\cdot (-1)^{4+3}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 1\\end{aligned}|-\\sqrt{5}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
